1790617776.743	
1790617776.743	In ancient civilizations like Mesopotamia (cuneiform tablets) and Egypt (hieroglyphic papyrus), duplication was achieved through painstaking transcription onto durable materials. The concept of *preservation* without rapid *multiplication* defined this era. The limitations were inherent in the medium itself—papyrus degrades, vellum is labor-intensive, and carving into stone or wood is inherently non-repeatable for high volume.
1790617776.743	
1790617776.743	The most significant technological precursor to modern printing emerged not in Europe, but across Asia. Early forms of block printing developed independently in China and Korea, centuries before the European invention achieved mass market penetration. These systems utilized **xylography** (woodblock printing).
